@0    = ~Welcome to the Seatower inn. There're food an' rooms available fo' those who can afford 'em. I am the inn master, Roryn th' name.~
@1    = ~I am very sorry for th' trouble and all th' li'l rabbits up there.~
@2    = ~How can I be o' service?~
@3    = ~Let me see what you have.~
@4    = ~You're a dwarf! I just had a... conversation with the cook about what she thinks of dwarves...~
@5    = ~About the rabbits...~
@6    = ~I won't go searching for your rats after all.~
@7    = ~I have a suspicion about the rats on the second floor. Is there any child currently staying here?~
@8    = ~Your daughter Azala... Does she have a pet animal, by any chance?~
@9    = ~I found a rat on the second floor...~
@10   = ~How is Azala?~
@11   = ~Nothing, good day.~
@12   = ~Ah, well, she's a good soul, ye know. We go t'gether really well—as long as I don' go into th' kitchen.~
@13   = ~But why did ye have t' kill a rabbit on the nice floor. Ach, I need to wipe the spot right away or it will give a stain. I'll not charge ye anything this time, but please, don't spill anything else inside the tower!~
@14   = ~I'll send Barney to take care of those right away, and then there's nothin' to remind us of tha' incident. Well, maybe after he also wiped th' floors.~
@15   = ~What was it with this mage? He looked rather desperate.~
@16   = ~I very much hope such an animal plague inside the rooms won't happen again!~
@17   = ~You do that, good man.~
@18   = ~Good day and have a pleasant stay at the Seatower inn!~
@19   = ~Och, me too, believe me that!~
@20   = ~How about I help you with collecting the rabbits?~
@21   = ~Well, I'll leave you to collecting those rabbits.~
@22   = ~Ach, that's Mr. Klaus. Came here some months ago, got kind o' trapped when the bridges went up. Always made a very frightened impression, if ye ask me. Constantly talks about an "Ulf" and that he's got to save 'im.~
@23   = ~Ulf? Is that a friend of his?~
@24   = ~Why rabbits?~
@25   = ~I believe so, yes, but he never told more. He wasn't always thinkin' clearly, if ye knows what I mean.~
@26   = ~I am pretty sure he tried to conjure somethin' else, but couldn't concentrate enough. Still, as long as it was a bunny or two, there was no real reason to kick 'im out of the inn before.~
@27   = ~Och! That would be a great help. Barney's still busy outside. Here's a cage t' put 'em in. I'll give you 50 gold for each o' those little hoppers, eh?~
@28   = ~Wha' of 'em?~
@29   = ~I'm done collecting, I think.~
@30   = ~I won't collect any more rabbits. Here's yer cage back.~
@31   = ~I won't collect them, after all.~
@32   = ~How many do ye think there are?~
@33   = ~I'll go back to chasing them.~
@34   = ~You do that! Barney is looking forward to collecting th' droppings.~
@35   = ~I see. Well, thanks anyway!~
@36   = ~So, how many ye've got?~
@37   = ~I see just the one. That's 50 gold, then!~
@38   = ~I see just the two. That's 100 gold, then!~
@39   = ~One, two...~
@40   = ~...three! That makes 150 gold, then.~
@41   = ~...four! That makes 200 gold, then.~
@42   = ~...five! That makes 250 gold, then.~
@43   = ~...six! That makes 300 gold, then.~
@44   = ~...seven! That makes 350 gold, then.~
@45   = ~...eight! That makes 400 gold, then.~
@46   = ~...nine! That makes 450 gold, then.~
@47   = ~...ten! That makes 500 gold, then.~
@48   = ~...eleven! That makes 550 gold, then.~
@49   = ~Here's yer gold, and thank ye for yer efforts!~
@50   = ~Oooh, so cute! Can I pet that one?~
@51   = ~Right? They are *really* cute!~
@52   = ~(Told you, <CHARNAME>!)~
@53   = ~Well, that they are, but...~
@54   = ~Lass, not now... I need to get them out of th' inn, and fast.~
@55   = ~Well, I'd say there are at least ten of 'em.~
@56   = ~Hullo!~
@57   = ~That's my little Azala.~
@58   = ~She's my daughter. My other two are ill and me wife, Dorna has her hands full nursing 'em, so I brought her to work with me. I hope she didn't do anything, she's usually quiet and no bother at all.~
@59   = ~(The rabbit is easy to catch. You could take it, but what would you do with a struggling rabbit in your backpack?)~
@60   = ~(The rabbit is easy to catch.)~
@61   = ~(You pick it up.)~
@62   = ~(You let it go.)~
@63   = ~(The rabbit hops away.)~
@64   = ~(You put it into the cage.)~
@65   = ~Oooh, they are *so* cute! Look at that fluffy, warm, little nose! I mean... would anyone even notice if one of them vanished? Hmmm?~
@66   = ~Puppy dog eyes won't work on me. The rabbits stay, Imoen.~
@67   = ~You want to take care of a normal bunny on our travels?~
@68   = ~Sure, I guess it would make a good dinner.~
@69   = ~But...~
@70   = ~Rabbits are gregarious animals, Imoen. Separating one is cruel.~
@71   = ~How, Imoen? How would you take care of it? Stuff it into your backpack? Put it onto a leash so it'll hurt itself? I will not watch a creature being tortured!~
@72   = ~Minsc has a hamster!~
@73   = ~... And lost him, at least for now. Ahem.~
@74   = ~Boo is not a normal hamster. He is a miniature giant space hamster, right, Boo?~
@75   = ~I'm all with Imoen. Let's catch three or four, it will be a great feast tonight! (Just make sure I'll get one to myself! There is plenty enough.)~
@76   = ~Awww, you really are a spoilsport. Fiiiiiiine.~
@77   = ~A rat! I saw a rat upstairs!~
@78   = ~Ma'am, we are very sorry for the inconvenience. Surely ye saw one of th' rabbits that were set free inside the house, but the situation is being...~
@79   = ~Excuse ME, do you think I can't tell a rat from a *rabbit*? I tell you, I saw a *rat* on the second floor of this very establishment.~
@80   = ~The second floor? Hm, thank you very much, m'lady. There were no rats in the cellar this morning, but if a guest is reporting a sighting, I need to send Barney up this instant.~
@81   = ~This reminds me of old days... I could kill those rats for you.~
@82   = ~Well, I won't have anything to do with this. Good luck to you.~
@83   = ~I understand ye well. Hey ho, Barney, grab the club and the spare traps and go fight those beasts!~
@84   = ~Azala, Mom sent a message for ye. Ye do not look well at all, go on home.~
@85   = ~I'm sure it's a rabbit. You have to look for a *rabbit*, and... and bring it to me!~
@86   = ~Maybe, lass, but if it's indeed rats, and not one of those hoppers that found the stairways, it needs to be dealt with quickly. Tell ye what, Barney's still busy upstairs swiping the floor of the hopper droppings. If ye went up one floor and see what ye find—I'll give ye 50 gold for every rat ye find before Barney gets to it. If it's a rabbit just let it hop, Barney will take care o' that later. Rats are more important. And, please, be ye as discreet as possible...~
@87   = ~No, Azala, not you. Stay with Poppa, please.~
@88   = ~Heh, yes, I think I remember old Reevor's rat plague. He was grumbling about your "bad" performance loud enough for half of Candlekeep to hear.~
@89   = ~Some things just have to be d-done.~
@90   = ~Rats do not belong inside a house but do endanger the health of the inhabitants. We do good by clearing them out.~
@91   = ~Lamenting about rats not belonging in houses. We should clear the coast of the whole tower and give it back to nature!~
@92   = ~There is honor even in the smallest errand.~
@93   = ~Yes, I guess it can be seen as an honorable task.~
@94   = ~I do think our time is better spent elsewhere, though.~
@95   = ~*I* will not touch a *rat*!~
@96   = ~Minsc is not too sure about this. They are small, furry animals, just like Boo...~
@97   = ~(squeak)~ [GAM_48]
@98   = ~Oh, Boo is right. Boo doesn't do number two in <CHARNAME>'s food. And he has no tail!~
@99   = ~This *has* to be a joke.~
@100  = ~Ah, yes, rats. Always a nuisance to keep out—of a shop just as well as an inn.~
@101  = ~To waste our power on vermin? Why don't we clear the whole inn, starting right here with this hall?!~
@102  = ~(The rat looks... shiny, as if it was washed and combed. It is also unusually fat. Upon taking a closer look, you discover a tiny little bow of pink ribbon on its tail.)~
@103  = ~(The rat is rather slow. You could grab it and take it with you.)~
@104  = ~(Grab the rat).~
@105  = ~(You let it wander off.)~
@106  = ~(You kill the rat.)~
@107  = ~(With a faint squeak the rat turns away.)~
@108  = ~(You place the rat in one of your pockets.)~
@109  = ~Ooh, look at those little whiskers! And the ribbon! She is so cute!~
@110  = ~Oh, I think this rat b-belongs to a child—looking at the ribbon on its tail.~
@111  = ~A *pet* rat. This is an abomination. The poor creature, it hasn't seen nature for a long time.~
@112  = ~I like the idea of a little furry pet rat. Although this one seems to belong to someone else.~
@113  = ~*Who* would take a *rat* as a pet?! Ewwwww!~
@114  = ~There are all sorts of ladies here with their offspring, who knows what those spoiled brats like to do to pass their time.~
@115  = ~Oh, you mean to anger the nanny? Hm... But a *rat*? Ewwwww!~
@116  = ~How about Minsc takes it and I get Boo?~
@117  = ~It will be a cold day in hell before I give up Boo!~
@118  = ~<CHARNAME> has to take care of a squeaking companion for whoever it belongs to now!~
@119  = ~Are you sure you have enough grain on you? These little guys like to eat.~
@120  = ~Garrick, this animal doesn't really look starved. I am sure it will do fine without anything to eat for a while. At least for as long as it takes <CHARNAME> to return it to its... "owner".~
@121  = ~We should find whoever claims this creature fast. This has wasted enough time already.~
@122  = ~How about we roast it and stuff it down the "owner's" neck? What a waste of time.~
@123  = ~How about we roast it and eat the good parts ourselves before returning it to the "owner." No need to let it go to waste.~
@124  = ~A pet rat in an inn... This has potential to become really entertaining. At least if I have a say in this.~
@125  = ~Pink ribbon? Combed fur? I bet all of <CHARNAME>'s gold she belongs to a girl.~
@126  = ~(You kill the rat with one stroke of your weapon and take the carcass with you.)~
@127  = ~Ooh, look at the ribbon! Looks like what I always did to Mr. Dolly. <CHARNAME>, I think you just made someone very sad.~
@128  = ~I think this rat b-belonged to a child—looking at the ribbon on its tail.~
@129  = ~A *pet* rat. This was an abomination. May the poor soul rest in peace.~
@130  = ~Oh? Well, there are some children around the main hall but none of them are staying in the rooms currently, so... Oh, and then there's me little Azala, at least today.~
@131  = ~We'll see about that. I'll bring you any rat I'll find.~
@132  = ~No, but funny ye should ask—she's been clamorin' for one for weeks.~
@133  = ~And I'll look forward to it. Well, not really, but ye know what I mean.~
@134  = ~Good! Just the one? Oh... it has a pink ribbon on its tail?!~
@135  = ~*screams* What?!~
@136  = ~What is it, Azala?~
@137  = ~Is she, is she really dead?~
@138  = ~What ye talking about, lass?~
@139  = ~*sob* The rat was my Betsy *sob*. I found her in the street last Friday and I nursed her and I didn't want to leave her alone at home so I took her with me and I let her run but only if she was not hungry and I took away the poop and I am sorry the lady saw her and I shouldn't have let her run but she escaped me and I hoped no-one would see her and then you told me to stay close and I couldn't go search for her and then I was sidetracked by all the rabbits and *takes breath*~
@140  = ~Ye brought a *rat* into the guest rooms? O' my inn? Azala, do ye have any idea what ye done?~
@141  = ~Betsy is dead, I confess. Azala, I am very sorry.~
@142  = ~Here's your rat. Can anyone take this carcass off me?~
@143  = ~She is not dead, Azala.~
@144  = ~Hush, Azala. I have her here, see?~
@145  = ~Really? After I just walked through more than ten rabbits upstairs, here's a child that wants a pet animal so desperately that she doesn't know what else to do but tame a *rat*?~
@146  = ~Whatever—can someone hush this child?~
@147  = ~Och. I'm sorry about this whole thing!~
@148  = ~*wails*~
@149  = ~Betsy! She's still alive! Oh, I was *so* scared you killed her! Thank you! Thank you!~
@150  = ~Azala, Mom sent a message for ye. Go on home. Take th' rat, an' we'll talk about this later.~
@151  = ~Well, this surely wasn't what I expected, but ye defeated one rat. Here is yer 50 gold.~
@152  = ~Thank ye for helping, although none of us expected things to go this way. Please enjoy yer stay at the Seatower Inn. And if ye have to chase another rodent through the guest rooms ye have a round on the house, I swear t' ye!~
@153  = ~Thank ye for asking. Don't ye worry about it. She'll have a pet rabbit. No, not a white one, saw too much o' those! The rat didn't sit well with us, anyway. There are limits t' good taste, as ye know well enough. I was sorry for her, she liked it so much, but it's better this way.~
